-- allium: 3
-- orchestrator: the main session holds this machine, and is the user's entry point: it plans the goal with them, picks the card up, chooses each move, triages what darts and drives turn up, and finishes or parks the work. It never writes code: every code change, an amend included, is a drive's; it may rewrite a commit's place or message. Darts and drives turn up the same kinds of thing, triaged alike.
-- Prior art: the goal is reasoned as a KAOS goal model (van Lamsweerde, Requirements Engineering, 2009); the route below it is the Mikado Method's.
-- Ports and adapters (Cockburn's hexagonal architecture): a need is named here as a plain verb — read the issue and its neighbourhood, search existing cards, update the tracker, file an issue, update the issue's description, comment on the issue, put a dependency onto another card, record in the session's journal, write the commit message (rewording included), weed the comments, raise a PR, write the work up on its issue, report where the session has got to, write the goal as a goal tree — and the project's instructions and installed skills bind each to what claims it.

use "./common.allium" as c

-- Record and conduct.
-- A user's question is a question, not a go-ahead: 'is that a good idea?' wants an answer, not the step it asks about.
-- Record in the session's journal, started in the session's plan file:
-- the process the session runs, `a-star:a-star`, so a session after a compaction reloads it; the mode, and each switch of it;
-- a sub-goal, with its kind, where it would be committed, what it depends on and whether a dart exercised it;
-- a decision (a call you aren't sure of is an open question), and an open question with what it blocks;
-- a sub-goal done, with its commits, and its `review:` entry (what drive.allium says it holds) against the commit's subject — the notes the drive agent wrote into the body, plus what the orchestrator's gate added;
-- what a drive's report taught, including where the road departed from the darts' route;
-- a revert or re-split as a `re-split:` entry, recording what changed and which sub-goals it superseded or reopened, and a replan as `replan:`;
-- what is in hand — the drive stack, innermost first — before the first edit; each main-bound tidy's pushed branch, until it lands; and a dart in flight, so a resumed session knows it was lost.
-- A decision that changes the problem or the agreed scope goes onto the issue now, at any stage, by updating its description, journalled as having gone; on an unfiled route it goes into the standalone PR where there is one, and otherwise stays in the journal. A dependency the session discovers goes onto the card it depends on, not this one. Triage and UserSettles apply this to every decision they record.
-- Every commit message — a drive's, a reword, a merged commit's, a split commit's, a re-sliced commit's, an amended commit's, a WIP commit's before it is pushed — is written by the need, write the commit message, and nothing is pushed or handed over with a message written otherwise. An amend's `fixup!` commit is the one exception, mechanical because the autosquash folds it away; the commit it lands in is reworded instead (drive.allium's `Committed`).
-- Keep the host's task list current: approving the plan adds a task per open leaf; a recorded sub-goal adds its task, replacing the leaf it refines; one done completes it; one superseded or thrown away removes it; a replan or re-split removes the tasks of the leaves it drops and adds those of the leaves it adds; and a frame interrupting a drive marks the drive's task blocked by its own.

-- Sub-goals.
-- Sub-goals come from darts, not from reading the code: the approved plan's leaves are provisional until a dart has exercised them, and harvesting refines each leaf it walked into sub-goals, replacing the leaf's task.
-- It is recorded already shaped as a review unit, so a proposed one that fails the review-unit tests is merged into the one it serves at harvest or triage, before the user is shown the split. A dart's split is evidence of what the route needs, never the commit plan as it stands.
-- Together a card's sub-goals complete it.

-- Planning. The session starts in plan mode, and nothing is built until the user approves the goal and the route.
-- `parked` is the session an earlier session parked on this card, restored from the record its park left; null where there is none.
-- The restore is guidance, not a rule: the model treats a parked Session as one entity across sessions, so its frames are already part of what `parked` carries, and rebuilding them from the park's comment is I/O, outside the model.
rule BeginPlanning {
    when: UserStartsCard(plan, mode, parked)
    requires: parked = null or parked.status = parked
    ensures:
        if parked != null:
            parked.mode = mode
            Recorded(session: parked, entry: process)
            SessionResumed(session: parked)
        else:
            let session = c/Session.created(
                plan: plan, mode: mode, picked_up: false,
                status: if plan.status = approved: choosing else: planning)
            Recorded(session: session, entry: process)
            if plan.status = approved:
                PickUpCard(session: session)

    @guidance
        -- Enter plan mode first — `EnterPlanMode`, or the host's equivalent — unless the session is already in it: exploring the problem is the planning, and plan mode holds every edit back until the user approves. This plan replaces the host's plan-mode template: no file lists, and sub-agents only where the exploration wants them.
        -- Read the problem and its neighbourhood, grounded in the repo and not the transcript. With a card: read the issue, its parent, its sub-issues, what blocks it and what it blocks, and its linked PRs. Without one: search the existing cards it might duplicate or belong under.
        -- Say what the scan tells you about this work being done now, and carry on; the issue's own why now, where it has one, is the reason, and work without one is opportunistic.
        -- Where the scan changes the picture — an open blocker, a duplicate, a card that supersedes this one, a card assigned to someone else — wait for the user's agreement before changing anything, the tracker included. Where the user named an umbrella, ask which piece; never pick the most plausible sub-task.
        -- A card an earlier session parked is not started afresh: its session is restored from the record its park left and resumed (Resume) — its plan, approved or not; whether the card was picked up; a revert in hand; the drive stack from the park's comment, innermost first, on the WIP head, so the frame in hand resumes first; and the unmerged main-bound tidies' branches the comment names. A comment that doesn't say whether the card was picked up predates the field, and is restored as picked up. Its branch is checked out, not recreated.
        -- Load the project's answers to the three choices below, from its skills and agent instructions first; only a choice answered nowhere there becomes a question, and an unanswered one is raised, not defaulted — bar branch cleanup (delete local branches and worktrees whose work has fully landed, offer to delete remote ones).
        -- The three: for a main-bound tidy's branch, what to verify; for the feature branch, what to verify, whether to push each commit and when a commit is done, which covers every commit on it, tidies that aren't main-bound included; and branch cleanup.
        -- Where a frame is committed, and that a main-bound tidy's branch is pushed and done once CI passes on it, are drive.allium's Placement paragraph, not the project's choices.
        -- Everything else follows the project's conventions: the tracker's, the base branch, ship/show/ask, the definition of done, coding standards and how a commit is written. What goes in a commit is a-star's: one atomic unit of change.
}

-- Readiness: the goal agreed with the user, and its reasoning done.
rule AgreeGoal {
    when: GoalAgreed(session)
    requires: session.status = planning
    ensures: session.plan.ready = true

    @guidance
        -- Agree the root with the user: what the card is for, never how it gets there. Ask what they can answer now rather than parking it; open questions keep only what nobody can answer yet. Report gaps — a missing goal, unclear scope, open questions nobody has answered — against what the issue should say.
        -- With a card, read its description and resolve its gaps in the issue, not in the plan.
        -- The session's output is an issue, or the implementation, and the plan takes that output's shape; ask where the request doesn't settle it. An issue: the plan is the issue body's draft, correcting the card's or drafting a new one. The implementation: the plan is what has to land, written as a goal tree, the goal at the root and what lands at the leaves. Either way it says what has to be true, not which files to touch; where the change reshapes the design, what has to be true is the target model, stated as a model diff at the root.
        -- The route is part of the plan: file and stop, implement an existing card, file and implement, or implement unfiled. With an existing card, file and stop corrects that card. Unfiled where a card would carry nothing the commit or a standalone PR can't. A route that implements predicts its finish, per the Finish rule's guidance, and MAY name its moves in order.
        -- The goal tree is a KAOS goal model. Each node is tested for sufficiency — complete refinement: its children, with the domain properties relied on, entail it within its constraints; a set of children that reaches the parent by breaking one of its constraints is not sufficient. A node whose children aren't clearly sufficient is marked `check:`. Say the domain properties out loud, and where sufficiency leans on what the user already knows about the system.
        -- To find a missing child, identify the parent's obstacles: ask what would stop it, rather than what would achieve it.
        -- A leaf is a requirement (something we do), an expectation (something expected of someone or something else: the user, CI, another team, an upstream library, existing behaviour) or a domain property (a plain fact relied on); say which where it isn't obvious. The kind is who is on the hook, apart from whether it is verified. An expectation never reads as a requirement: it sits in the tree looking like work, and nothing happens until someone notices it was never assigned.
        -- A gap is an obstacle and invalidates everything above it: surface it. Close it by a deliberate choice of resolution tactic, never by reflexively adding a task: goal substitution, agent substitution, obstacle prevention, obstacle reduction, goal restoration, obstacle mitigation, goal weakening or obstacle tolerance. Goal weakening and obstacle tolerance leave no trace in the tree, so record each in Out of scope or Alternative approaches: scope deliberately cut is a decision someone will want the reasoning for.
}

-- Approving is the user leaving plan mode. Where the plan implements, the session picks the card up and takes the plan's first move; a plan that only files an issue files it and stops.
rule ApprovePlan {
    when: UserApproves(session)
    requires: session.status = planning
    requires: session.plan.ready
    requires: session.plan.output = implementation
    ensures: session.plan.status = approved
    ensures: session.status = choosing
    ensures:
        if not session.picked_up:
            PickUpCard(session: session)
        else if session.plan.route.count > 0:
            Take(session: session, move: session.plan.route.first)
        else:
            MoveDue(session: session)

    @guidance
        -- `ExitPlanMode`, or the host's equivalent, asks for the approval; never ask for it any other way, and never approve your own plan: a secondary session starts implementing only from a plan the user approved.
        -- Asking is a claim that the plan is aligned, not that it is finished, so answer three questions first: is any part of the plan a guess you'd revise if you asked the user one question; have you taken a neutral acknowledgement for agreement; is there a decision inside the plan you made without surfacing it? Yes to any: ask that instead, and stay in plan mode. Uncertain: stay in plan mode — exiting is the one move within the turn that can't be taken back.
        -- A rejection with comments is planning continuing: revise the plan, record what changed as decisions, each one it replaces marked `superseded by D<m>`, and ask again.
        -- The first move, once picked up, is the first the approved plan names, taken directly; otherwise a dart where the card has no sub-goals yet or part of its goal is unwalked, else a drive.
}

rule FileAndStop {
    when: UserApproves(session)
    requires: session.status = planning
    requires: session.plan.ready
    requires: session.plan.output = issue
    ensures: session.plan.status = approved
    ensures: session.status = exited
    ensures: session.exit = filed
    ensures: SessionEnded(session: session)

    @guidance
        -- File the issue, or with an existing card update its description.
}

-- Pickup is the approved plan's first step, never done while planning: plan mode is read-only, and until the plan is approved nobody has agreed to do the work.
-- PickUpCard asks for it; the attempt ends in CardPickedUp, or, where the tracker conventions leave a question in secondary, PickUpBlocked (PickUpParks).
rule PickUp {
    when: CardPickedUp(session)
    requires: session.status = choosing
    requires: not session.picked_up
    ensures: session.picked_up = true
    ensures:
        if session.plan.route.count > 0:
            Take(session: session, move: session.plan.route.first)
        else:
            MoveDue(session: session)

    @guidance
        -- With a card, update the tracker first: the user as assignee, then status and board as the project's tracker conventions say for a card being picked up; where they don't say, ask.
        -- Then create or check out the feature branch off the project's base branch, named by the project's convention.
        -- A file-and-implement route files the issue first. A plan re-approved after a replan goes back to implementing without picking up, or filing, again (ApprovePlan).
}

-- In secondary, a pickup the tracker conventions leave a question on parks with the card not picked up, so the session that resumes it picks it up again (Resume).
rule PickUpParks {
    when: PickUpBlocked(session)
    requires: session.status = choosing
    requires: not session.picked_up
    requires: session.mode = secondary
    ensures: session.status = parked
    ensures: Recorded(session: session, entry: open_question)

    @guidance
        -- Park as TakePark says.
}

-- A switch is the user's, never the session's own; the session stays secondary until they switch it back explicitly, and a message while it runs doesn't change the mode.
-- A move on offer when the user switches to secondary is chosen again under it.
rule SwitchMode {
    when: UserSwitchesMode(session, mode)
    requires: session.mode != mode
    ensures: session.mode = mode
    ensures: Recorded(session: session, entry: mode)
    ensures:
        if mode = secondary and session.status = offered:
            session.status = choosing
            Choose(session: session, move: session.offered_move)

    @guidance
        -- Coming back from secondary, record the mode, then refresh the user's context by reporting where the session has got to, leading with the decisions made while they were away and the questions left for them.
}

-- A secondary session with no approved plan plans, records its questions and parks, the draft plan recorded.
rule ParkDraft {
    when: PlanDrafted(session)
    requires: session.status = planning
    requires: session.mode = secondary
    ensures: session.status = parked
    ensures: Recorded(session: session, entry: draft_plan)

    @guidance
        -- Park as TakePark says, where a branch with work exists — a replan after work; otherwise the draft goes into the comment on the issue.
}

-- Every entry into choosing takes a move or chooses one, bar the user's no (DeclineOffer), which leaves the choice with them: ApprovePlan, PickUp, Resume, RevertDone and FinishReopens raise MoveDue where they take none.
rule ChooseMove {
    when: MoveDue(session)
    requires: session.status = choosing
    -- Which move is the orchestrator's choice, per ApprovePlan's guidance; a drive where one can be taken, else a dart, stands for it.
    ensures: Choose(session: session, move:
        if session.open_frames.count = 0 and session.drivable_sub_goals.count > 0: drive else: dart)
}

-- A move the approved plan's route names is taken in the route's order without being offered: approving the plan was the yes.
-- No drive or revert is offered or taken before the card is picked up, here, in OfferMove or in TakeUnoffered; PickUp chooses again once it is.
rule TakeOnRoute {
    when: ChooseOnRoute(session, move)
    requires: session.status = choosing
    requires: session.picked_up or (move != drive and move != revert)
    ensures: Take(session: session, move: move)
}

rule OfferMove {
    when: Choose(session, move)
    requires: session.status = choosing
    requires: session.mode = primary
    requires: session.picked_up or (move != drive and move != revert)
    ensures: session.status = offered
    ensures: session.offered_move = move

    @guidance
        -- Primary: the user is watching and steers as the session goes, so the session manages their context — they see only what their current thread needs, and the session raises everything else at the right time. Escalate what the user should decide and decide the rest yourself; every question costs them context.
        -- Interruption management: defer to breakpoints (Iqbal & Bailey), with the session mediating (McFarlane), and GTD's inbox, open loops and review. A thread is the topic the user's last message engages with, and its close is the breakpoint; it lasts until a decision is reached, the user drops it, or they change the subject. Several questions raised open one thread each: the one the user answers is wrapped up first, and the rest stay open loops.
        -- Mid-thread, an arrival — a sub-agent's report, a gate, a test run, CI, a weeder's output, a move to offer — goes to the journal as an `inbox:` entry. A reply inside the thread carries no 'meanwhile…' tail; a turn woken only by an arrival writes at most one short line, never the arrival's content; work an arrival unblocks goes on silently where it needs no yes.
        -- An arrival breaks into the thread only where it materially affects it — it breaks a premise the user is reasoning from, or answers the question under discussion — framed as what it changes for the thread. You judge materiality, never the user: asking would itself interrupt.
        -- At the breakpoint raise the inbox triaged, not in arrival order: decisions, with the pending move offers batched into one; what changes the user's picture; then the open loops, and one line for the rest. With no thread open, raise each arrival as it lands, under the same triage.
        -- A move off the plan's route is offered then; a dart's route question waits for the user like any other arrival, unless it is yours to answer.
        -- What is the user's — plan approval, a reversed finish, a question only they can answer — is offered, and taken only on their yes.
}

-- Only the user's yes takes the offered move. A question from the user is not a yes.
rule AcceptOffer {
    when: UserSaysYes(session)
    requires: session.status = offered
    ensures: session.status = choosing
    ensures: Take(session: session, move: session.offered_move)
}

rule DeclineOffer {
    when: UserSaysNo(session)
    requires: session.status = offered
    ensures: session.status = choosing
}

rule TakeUnoffered {
    when: Choose(session, move)
    requires: session.status = choosing
    requires: session.mode = secondary
    requires: session.picked_up or (move != drive and move != revert)
    ensures: Take(session: session, move: move)
    ensures: Recorded(session: session, entry: would_have_offered)

    @guidance
        -- Secondary: the user isn't watching, so nobody answers a question until they're back. Each call is halt, decide or assume: record each decision, and each point you carry on past as an assumption. Halt a line of work only on a show-stopper, or a change that would move the outcome significantly from the plan.
        -- In secondary, work only on a ready card: one that isn't, or a question the card can't answer, gets its questions recorded and the card parked.
        -- A halt is recorded as an open question blocking that sub-goal, and other ready sub-goals go on; with none left, park. Merging stays the user's. An open question blocks a sub-goal only where it is a halt: one whose plan routes around it stays ready.
        -- Push a drive's branch after each frame's gate wherever the project's answers allow, so CI backstops what nobody is watching.
}

-- dart: part of the goal not yet walked, or a sub-goal no dart has exercised, that nothing in flight covers.
rule TakeDart {
    when: Take(session, move)
    requires: move = dart
    requires: session.status = choosing
    ensures: c/Dart.created(session: session, status: briefed, still_needed: true)
    ensures: Recorded(session: session, entry: dart_in_flight)

    @guidance
        -- Darts run in parallel with each other and with a drive.
        -- Brief the dart agent with the card as its whole brief: the goal, invariants and out of scope from the tracker issue, the sub-goals already found, the decisions in force and the record's `principle:` entries, plus the branch to start from and its head — the card's feature branch, else the base — or point it at where the card's work is recorded, to read and never write.
        -- Ask for a spike: the route built, not analysed, reporting as soon as the next steps are decidable. Never ask for a green suite, the definition of done or proof of the end-to-end.
        -- Spawn it with `isolation: "worktree"`, in the background so it can message you. `.claude/worktrees/` must be ignored by git; add it to `$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/info/exclude` where it isn't, as `.git` is a file in a linked worktree.
        -- Every dart starts fresh from the current base — or from another dart's branch where what it needs isn't committed yet. An old dart is evidence, never resumed.
        -- A dart branch is local: neither you nor the agent pushes it.
}

-- drive: one drivable sub-goal, recorded as in hand before the first edit.
rule TakeDrive {
    when: Take(session, move)
    requires: move = drive
    requires: session.status = choosing
    requires: session.picked_up
    requires: session.open_frames.count = 0
    requires: session.drivable_sub_goals.count > 0
    -- Which drivable sub-goal is taken is the orchestrator's choice; `.first` stands for it.
    let sub_goal = session.drivable_sub_goals.first
    ensures: sub_goal.status = in_hand
    ensures: c/Frame.created(session: session, sub_goal: sub_goal, kind: sub_goal.kind, placement: sub_goal.placement, status: in_hand)
    ensures: Recorded(session: session, entry: in_hand)

    @guidance
        -- A sub-goal is drivable once a dart has exercised it — built and run, not reasoned about — or once you judge it straightforward (JudgeStraightforward).
        -- Record it as in hand before anything is edited.
        -- Record the branch's head, check out the branch the frame is committed on — a main-bound tidy's own branch for that tidy or an amend of it, else the feature branch — then spawn the drive agent in the background with the frame as its brief and nothing else: sub-goal, kind, placement, branch, head, and where the card's work is recorded. Do not restate what it loads for itself — the project's conventions, how to verify — nor dictate a format the conventions own, such as a commit message's. It works in the card's own checkout, with no worktree; do not touch it until the agent ends. A harness hook reporting uncommitted changes meanwhile is reporting the frame's work in progress.
        -- Triage what it reports without stopping the frame. It spawns the sub-agents the conventions call for itself; where it has no Agent tool it asks you, and you spawn each with exactly the inputs it names and resume it. It asks you the questions the commit conventions would put to the user: answer from the record, or put it to the user.
        -- When it ends its turn: spawn what it asked for; offer a user's decision in primary; push an amend frame first where an earlier commit can't take this frame; for a callers-check miss outside the frame's scope, apply the callers check's remedy — an amend frame of the first caller where it is committed, or its sub-goal where it isn't, resuming the agent to drop the additions; a revert where the move can't fix it, which ends the frame; take a better drive as a pre-emption; handle a failure per Fail. Then resume it with SendMessage, or push the new frame.
        -- Gate the frame's diff from the recorded head before taking its commit: check the review notes the drive agent wrote into the commit's body, and reported, stand apart from the unlanded commits' `review:` entries; a tidy that shows a behaviour change, error paths included, is split or retyped; the diff is the brief's sub-goal at its placement; every commit an amend landed in has its message written again through write the commit message, against its final diff; the branch still reads in review units; the callers check holds. A miss inside the frame's scope resumes the agent to fix it; anything else is triaged: a callers-check miss gets the callers check's remedy, and one that completes an earlier unlanded commit becomes an amend of it.
        -- Take its commit, rewriting its place or message where the branch should read otherwise: write the commit message again only where the review notes in its body fall short of a `review:` entry. A rewrite that changes a subject updates the journal entries keyed by it. Push where the project's answer says to, and always push a main-bound frame's tidy branch and wait for CI to pass on it before taking the commit (drive.allium's `Committed`); the agent never pushes.
        -- Record the sub-goal done and record the commit's `review:` entry, against its subject, with what your check added — the PR's reviewing notes take what spans commits and what was verified from these. A frame that interrupted another pops, rebasing the WIP onto it and resuming what it interrupted; a main-bound tidy leaves its pushed branch and the feature branch as drive.allium's Placement paragraph says. A dart branch under the new commit is the dart's to rebase or redo, its call. Clear the darts this drive superseded: record what each taught that the record doesn't hold, then remove its branch and worktree.
        -- Hand over the branch, its head and what the reviewer has to check; in secondary the handover goes in the session's record and doesn't end the turn. Then you are back to choosing. Merging is the user's; once they have, remove what only served it per the project's branch cleanup.
}

-- revert: what is now known changes the card's target, while its goal still stands.
rule TakeRevert {
    when: Take(session, move)
    requires: move = revert
    requires: session.status = choosing
    requires: session.picked_up
    ensures: session.status = reverting
    ensures: session.revert_in_hand = true
    ensures: Recorded(session: session, entry: re_split)
    ensures:
        for frame in session.open_frames:
            frame.status = failed
            frame.sub_goal.status = if frame.sub_goal.is_ready: ready else: pending

    @guidance
        -- Tag the old head, restart from the base, and replay the commits that still hold; frames in hand are thrown away, their WIP kept on the old head by the tag.
        -- A main-bound tidy the revert replays stays main-bound.
        -- A revert is a move of its own, not a failure path: what the branch cost so far is no argument against it. It is journalled as a re-split and never re-enters plan mode.
}

-- A done sub-goal whose commit was not replayed is outstanding again; one the new target no longer needs is then superseded.
rule RevertDone {
    when: Replayed(session, not_replayed)
    requires: session.status = reverting
    ensures: session.status = choosing
    ensures: session.revert_in_hand = false
    ensures: MoveDue(session: session)
    ensures:
        for sub_goal in not_replayed:
            sub_goal.status = if sub_goal.is_ready: ready else: pending
            TaskAdded(sub_goal: sub_goal)
}

-- replan: the goal itself is invalidated. The session re-enters plan mode, and nothing more is built until the user approves the revised plan.
rule TakeReplan {
    when: Take(session, move)
    requires: move = replan
    requires: session.status = choosing
    ensures: session.status = planning
    ensures: session.plan.status = drafting
    ensures: session.plan.ready = false
    ensures: Recorded(session: session, entry: replan)
    ensures:
        for frame in session.open_frames:
            frame.status = failed
            frame.sub_goal.status = pending
    ensures:
        for dart in session.darts where status = running or status = waiting:
            dart.status = lost
            Recorded(session: session, entry: dart_lost)
    ensures:
        for y in session.untriaged:
            if y.kind = route_question or y.kind = user_decision:
                Recorded(session: session, entry: open_question)
            y.triaged = true

    @guidance
        -- Stop the running dart and drive agents first; the drive agent edits the card's checkout.
        -- Frames in hand are thrown away with the plan they served; their WIP commits stay as evidence for the re-plan. Record in the `replan:` entry what each lost dart found, and what each untriaged yield of the old plan says, that could feed the re-plan; the yields are then discarded, each question among them kept as an open question with what it blocks.
        -- Re-enter plan mode before any other step, naming in the `replan:` entry what the goal's change invalidated, marked and not deleted; revise the plan and ask for approval as in ApprovePlan.
}

-- park: nothing is ready, and what is blocked waits on the user or someone else. The session ends leaving what TakePark's guidance says.
rule TakePark {
    when: Take(session, move)
    requires: move = park
    requires: session.status = choosing
    requires: session.ready_sub_goals.count = 0
    ensures: session.status = parked
    ensures:
        for frame in session.in_hand:
            frame.status = parked

    @guidance
        -- A park leaves what a later session needs to resume, where the project tracks the card. In this order: write the work up on its issue; push the feature branch with `--force-with-lease`, WIP commits included once their messages are written, leaving a branch the session doesn't own as it is and saying so; then comment on the issue with the branch, its head commit's subject, whether the card was picked up, the unmerged main-bound tidies' branches, and what was in hand — the drive stack, innermost first, or a revert with the tag on its old head.
        -- Stopping mid-drive WIP-commits the work and leaves it recorded as in hand. A park with no branch yet — a draft plan — carries the draft in that comment instead.
}

-- Stopping: the user ends the session mid-work, whatever else is ready. The stack is parked as it stands.
rule Stop {
    when: UserStops(session)
    requires: session.status = planning or session.status = choosing or session.status = offered
        or session.status = reverting or session.status = finishing
    ensures: session.status = parked
    ensures:
        for frame in session.in_hand:
            frame.status = parked

    @guidance
        -- Park as TakePark says.
}

-- One rule resumes parked work, whether the parked session continues or a later session starts on the card, which BeginPlanning restores from the park's record on the issue and resumes here.
-- A session stopped mid-revert resumes reverting, so RevertDone completes the revert; otherwise an approved plan resumes choosing, picking the card up only where the park left it not picked up, and the frame in hand resumes writing with the rest of the stack still under it; otherwise the plan resumes planning.
rule Resume {
    when: SessionResumed(session)
    requires: session.status = parked
    ensures: session.status =
        if session.revert_in_hand: reverting
        else if session.plan.status = approved: choosing
        else: planning
    ensures:
        if not session.revert_in_hand and session.plan.status = approved:
            if not session.picked_up:
                PickUpCard(session: session)
            else if session.open_frames.count = 0:
                MoveDue(session: session)
    ensures:
        for frame in session.frames where status = parked:
            frame.status = writing
}

-- A dart in flight when the session ends or parks — exited, parked, or the conversation itself gone — is lost,
-- and darted again by whichever session picks the work up, where still needed.
rule DartLost {
    when: SessionEnded(session)
    for dart in session.darts where status = running or status = waiting:
        ensures: dart.status = lost
        ensures: Recorded(session: session, entry: dart_lost)
}

rule DartLostOnPark {
    when: session: c/Session.status transitions_to parked
    for dart in session.darts where status = running or status = waiting:
        ensures: dart.status = lost
        ensures: Recorded(session: session, entry: dart_lost)

    @guidance
        -- Stop the running dart agents before the park's comment on the issue, and carry in it what each lost dart found that the record doesn't hold.
}

-- Before the done exit, the review-unit pass shapes the unlanded branch into review units.
rule ShapeForReview {
    when: ReviewUnitsChecked(session)
    requires: session.status = choosing
    requires: session.open_frames.count = 0
    ensures: session.in_review_units = true
    ensures: Recorded(session: session, entry: review_units)

    @guidance
        -- Shape the unlanded branch for its reviewer, not the order the work happened in: reorder, merge, split or re-slice commits until each is independently acceptable and none is a slice of another, leaving the final tree unchanged.
        -- Main-bound tidies stay at the bottom of the branch, their pushed tidy branches kept in step.
        -- A re-slice moves scope from one commit to another along a slice boundary, and keeps the number of commits the same.
        -- A re-slice never moves scope beneath anything it needs, or above anything that needs it.
        -- The test is the `review:` entries: a commit whose entry can't say what checking it means apart from its neighbours' is a slice of one of them, and an entry that needs two different checks is two units — so a tidy and an advance never share a commit.
        -- Run the callers check on every commit once the branch is shaped, and apply the callers check's remedy to each miss.
        -- The pass ends here only once no miss is left whose remedy is a revert or a new amend frame; such a miss is ShapeFindsMiss's.
        -- The slicing rules, the callers check and the completion test are in drive.allium.
        -- Merging two commits merges their `review:` entries and writes the merged commit message; splitting one splits its `review:` entry and writes each part's message; re-slicing scope between two commits rewrites both their `review:` entries and writes both messages; a changed subject updates the journal entries keyed by it.
}

-- A callers-check miss the review-unit pass can't fix by moving additions leaves the branch unshaped: a revert is chosen, or an amend of the first caller is recorded, and the pass runs again once it is done.
-- The pass raises ShapingFoundMiss for such a miss instead of ReviewUnitsChecked, which it raises only with none left; needs_revert is the callers check's remedy being a revert.
rule ShapeFindsMiss {
    when: ShapingFoundMiss(session, needs_revert, first_caller)
    requires: session.status = choosing
    requires: session.open_frames.count = 0
    ensures: session.in_review_units = false
    ensures:
        if needs_revert:
            Choose(session: session, move: revert)
        else:
            let sub_goal = c/SubGoal.created(
                session: session, status: pending, kind: amend, placement: inside_commit,
                amends: first_caller, exercised: false, judged_straightforward: false)
            Recorded(session: session, entry: sub_goal)
            TaskAdded(sub_goal: sub_goal)
}

-- After the review-unit pass, the code review runs once over the whole unlanded branch.
-- The orchestrator runs it and triages its findings; a drive commits each as an amend, so a finding resets code_reviewed through Committed.
rule ReviewBranch {
    when: BranchCodeReviewed(session)
    requires: session.status = choosing
    requires: session.open_frames.count = 0
    requires: session.in_review_units
    ensures: session.code_reviewed = true
    ensures: Recorded(session: session, entry: code_review)

    @guidance
        -- Run the review at the level the project's conventions set — the review they name, or a code-review agent — over the code and its comments.
        -- Each finding is triaged like any report and driven as an amend of the commit it belongs to, never fixed by you; one may be driven without a dart once judged straightforward (JudgeStraightforward).
        -- A finding a drive could only have missed because something it reads was silent or wrong is also a gap: fix it where it belongs — the project's instructions or skills, the record, a skill of a-star's, or the session's setup.
        -- Amends reopen both passes: rerun the review-unit pass, and the code review over what the amends changed, until a pass turns up nothing to drive.
}

rule TakeDone {
    when: Take(session, move)
    requires: move = done
    requires: session.status = choosing
    requires: session.open_frames.count = 0
    requires: session.sub_goals.all(s => s.status = done or s.status = superseded)
    requires: session.in_review_units
    requires: session.code_reviewed
    ensures: session.status = finishing

    @guidance
        -- Done needs the sub-goals to complete the card, the branch to have had its review-unit pass, and the code review run once over the whole unlanded branch.
}

-- In secondary, a PR the approved plan didn't predict — any PR where it predicted none, or the other kind of PR than the one it predicted — is never opened: the reversal that calls for one is recorded as an open question for the user, and the finish takes no PR.
-- A reversal away from a predicted PR stays the session's to decide (Finish).
rule FinishWithholdsPR {
    when: FinishCallsForUnpredictedPR(session)
    requires: session.status = finishing
    requires: session.mode = secondary
    ensures: Recorded(session: session, entry: open_question)
    ensures: FinishDecided(session: session, outcome: no_pr, user_agreed: false)

    @guidance
        -- Record the PR the evidence calls for and its grounds, then finish as no_pr says; the user raises the PR, or not, once they're back.
}

-- The finish decision, made with the user: a PR or not. A PR exists to share what the commit doesn't, so the prediction follows where that knowledge lives.
rule Finish {
    when: FinishDecided(session, outcome, user_agreed)
    requires: session.status = finishing
    requires: outcome = session.plan.finish or user_agreed or (session.mode = secondary and outcome = no_pr)
    ensures: session.status = exited
    ensures: session.exit = done
    ensures: Recorded(session: session, entry: finish)
    ensures:
        if outcome != session.plan.finish:
            Recorded(session: session, entry: finish_reversal)
    ensures: SessionEnded(session: session)

    @guidance
        -- Ask the user only where the work reverses the plan's prediction. In secondary, decide: record the reversal and its grounds, and take the outcome the evidence selects, where it selects one and it is the plan's or no PR. A reversal calling for a PR the plan didn't predict is the user's (FinishWithholdsPR). Opening a PR the plan predicted is allowed; merging it never is.
        -- The outcome is the first whose condition holds, in this order; where none holds, ask the user, and in secondary park (FinishParks).
        -- no_pr, where the commit carries it all, or an issue carries the knowledge and nothing in the implementation is counter-intuitive: first update the issue's description where the work changed what it should say. The finish ends with a report naming the branch, its head sha and the unmerged main-bound tidies' branches.
        -- standalone_pr, where there is no issue and something to share beyond the commit: raise a PR that carries the problem itself.
        -- pr_on_issue, where the implementation is worth sharing in its own right: raise a PR that resolves the issue, or is part of it.
        -- Push the branch with `--force-with-lease`, then raise the PR. The finish doesn't end while a blocking question on the PR is unsettled; in secondary it is left open on the PR, marked for the user, and the finish ends.
}

-- A finish that turns up an amend — CI failing, a finding on the PR — goes back to choosing: the amend is triaged and driven, and the finish is decided again.
rule FinishReopens {
    when: FinishFoundAmend(session)
    requires: session.status = finishing
    ensures: session.status = choosing
    ensures: MoveDue(session: session)
}

-- A reword the finish turns up changes no code, so it needs no triage or drive: the finish applies it in place and carries on.
rule FinishRewords {
    when: FinishFoundReword(session)
    requires: session.status = finishing
    ensures: Recorded(session: session, entry: reword)

    @guidance
        -- Write the message through the need, write the commit message; update the journal entries keyed by a subject it changes; push with `--force-with-lease` where the branch is already on the remote.
}

-- In secondary, a finish no outcome fits is parked, not asked.
rule FinishParks {
    when: FinishUndecidable(session)
    requires: session.status = finishing
    requires: session.mode = secondary
    ensures: session.status = parked
    ensures: Recorded(session: session, entry: open_question)

    @guidance
        -- Park as TakePark says.
}

rule SubGoalReady {
    when: sub_goal: c/SubGoal.is_ready
    requires: sub_goal.status = pending
    ensures: sub_goal.status = ready
}

-- One with nothing left to depend on is ready from the start; its is_ready never turns from false to true.
rule SubGoalReadyOnCreation {
    when: sub_goal: c/SubGoal.created
    requires: sub_goal.status = pending
    requires: sub_goal.is_ready
    ensures: sub_goal.status = ready
}

-- A failed drive's sub-goal, re-split, is superseded by the sub-goals that replace it.
rule Supersede {
    when: OrchestratorSupersedes(sub_goal)
    requires: sub_goal.status = pending or sub_goal.status = ready
    ensures: sub_goal.status = superseded
    ensures: TaskRemoved(sub_goal: sub_goal)
    ensures: Recorded(session: sub_goal.session, entry: re_split)
}

-- A failed drive's sub-goal stays pending until the orchestrator readies it again, or a re-split supersedes it (Supersede).
rule SubGoalReReadied {
    when: OrchestratorReadies(sub_goal)
    requires: sub_goal.status = pending
    requires: sub_goal.is_ready
    ensures: sub_goal.status = ready
}

-- A sub-goal no dart exercised is driven only once the orchestrator judges it straightforward, recorded as a decision: an amend raised by the code review, say.
rule JudgeStraightforward {
    when: JudgedStraightforward(sub_goal)
    requires: sub_goal.status = pending or sub_goal.status = ready
    requires: not sub_goal.exercised
    ensures: sub_goal.judged_straightforward = true
    ensures: Recorded(session: sub_goal.session, entry: decision)
}

-- Triage.
-- A yield is triaged from choosing; one arriving while a move is offered, a revert runs or the session is parked waits.
-- One at a time: a yield that chooses a move may leave choosing, and the next waits until it is back.
rule TriageOnArrival {
    when: y: c/Yield.created
    requires: y.session.status = choosing
    requires: y.session.untriaged.first = y
    ensures: Triage(y: y)
}

rule TriageNext {
    when: y: c/Yield.triaged becomes true
    requires: y.session.status = choosing
    requires: y.session.untriaged.count > 0
    ensures: Triage(y: y.session.untriaged.first)
}

rule TriageBacklog {
    when: session: c/Session.status transitions_to choosing
    requires: session.untriaged.count > 0
    ensures: Triage(y: session.untriaged.first)
}

rule TriageGoalChange {
    when: Triage(y)
    requires: y.changes_goal
    ensures: y.triaged = true
    ensures: Choose(session: y.session, move: replan)
    ensures:
        if y.from_dart != null:
            DartReport(dart: y.from_dart)
}

rule TriageTargetChange {
    when: Triage(y)
    requires: not y.changes_goal
    requires: y.changes_target
    ensures: y.triaged = true
    ensures: Choose(session: y.session, move: revert)
    ensures:
        if y.from_dart != null:
            DartReport(dart: y.from_dart)
}

rule TriageUnwalkedRoute {
    when: Triage(y)
    requires: y.kind = unwalked_route
    requires: not y.changes_goal and not y.changes_target
    ensures: y.triaged = true
    ensures: Recorded(session: y.session, entry: unwalked_route)
    ensures: Choose(session: y.session, move: dart)

    @guidance
        -- A stretch of the route not yet walked is darted.
}

rule TriageSubGoal {
    when: Triage(y)
    requires: y.kind = sub_goal or y.kind = increment
    requires: not y.changes_goal and not y.changes_target
    requires: y.unlanded_superseded.count = 0
    requires: y.completes = null
    ensures: y.triaged = true
    ensures:
        let sub_goal = c/SubGoal.created(
            session: y.session, status: pending, kind: y.frame_kind, placement: y.placement,
            exercised: y.exercised, judged_straightforward: false)
        Recorded(session: y.session, entry: sub_goal)
        TaskAdded(sub_goal: sub_goal)

    @guidance
        -- Apply the completion test first, for scope the user adds mid-card and findings from the pre-landing code review as much as a dart's or drive's: scope that completes an unlanded commit is an amend of it (TriageCompletion).
        -- A better split of the sub-goal a frame is driving is a pre-emption, not a new sub-goal alongside it.
}

-- New scope that completes an unlanded commit is an amend of that commit, not a commit of its own:
-- a reviewer would otherwise accept a slice, then the rest of the same change.
rule TriageCompletion {
    when: Triage(y)
    requires: y.kind = sub_goal or y.kind = increment
    requires: not y.changes_goal and not y.changes_target
    requires: y.unlanded_superseded.count = 0
    requires: y.completes != null and not y.completes.landed
    ensures: y.triaged = true
    ensures:
        let sub_goal = c/SubGoal.created(
            session: y.session, status: pending, kind: amend, placement: inside_commit,
            amends: y.completes, exercised: y.exercised, judged_straightforward: false)
        Recorded(session: y.session, entry: sub_goal)
        TaskAdded(sub_goal: sub_goal)
}

rule TriageSupersedingChange {
    when: Triage(y)
    requires: y.kind = sub_goal or y.kind = increment
    requires: not y.changes_goal and not y.changes_target
    requires: y.unlanded_superseded.count > 0
    ensures: y.triaged = true
    ensures:
        if y.unlanded_superseded.count = 1:
            for commit in y.unlanded_superseded:
                let sub_goal = c/SubGoal.created(
                    session: y.session, status: pending, kind: amend, placement: inside_commit,
                    amends: commit, exercised: y.exercised, judged_straightforward: false)
                Recorded(session: y.session, entry: sub_goal)
                TaskAdded(sub_goal: sub_goal)
        else:
            Choose(session: y.session, move: revert)

    @guidance
        -- A change replacing the approach of an unlanded commit anywhere in the branch is an amend of that commit, or a revert where it spans several.
}

rule TriageRecordOnly {
    when: Triage(y)
    requires: y.kind = constraint
    requires: not y.changes_goal and not y.changes_target
    ensures: y.triaged = true
    ensures: Recorded(session: y.session, entry: on_goal_tree_node)

    @guidance
        -- A constraint, assumption or risk is recorded on the goal-tree node it bounds.
}

-- An earlier, unlanded commit is amended; before the frame in hand goes on only where that frame can't be committed correctly without it.
rule TriageEarlierCommit {
    when: Triage(y)
    requires: y.kind = earlier_commit
    requires: not y.changes_goal and not y.changes_target
    ensures: y.triaged = true
    ensures:
        let sub_goal = c/SubGoal.created(
            session: y.session, status: pending, kind: amend, placement: inside_commit,
            amends: y.commit, exercised: y.exercised, judged_straightforward: false)
        Recorded(session: y.session, entry: sub_goal)
        TaskAdded(sub_goal: sub_goal)
        if y.blocks_frame and y.from_frame != null and y.from_frame.status = writing:
            PushFrame(frame: y.from_frame, sub_goal: sub_goal)

    @guidance
        -- An amend is pushed ahead of the frame in hand only where that frame can't be committed correctly on the commit as it stands; otherwise it is recorded to drive after it.
        -- From a dart, a frame no longer writing, or a frame the commit doesn't block, it stays a recorded sub-goal, driven when ready.
}

rule TriageRouteQuestion {
    when: Triage(y)
    requires: y.kind = route_question
    requires: not y.changes_goal and not y.changes_target
    ensures: y.triaged = true
    ensures: Recorded(session: y.session, entry: decision)
    ensures:
        if y.from_dart != null:
            OrchestratorResumes(dart: y.from_dart)

    @guidance
        -- Answer it where it is yours; otherwise it is the user's, asked per OfferMove in primary and recorded as an open question per TakeUnoffered in secondary. Record the answer as a decision, which reaches the issue as Record and conduct says.
        -- Two reports that conflict are yours to settle, by your own check where one is cheap, and recorded as a decision.
        -- A report that its brief breaks one of the frame's standards is a wrong brief: re-brief within the standard or put the conflict to the user, never record the standard as waived.
}

-- The user's to settle, in either mode: asked in primary, raised at the thread's close per OfferMove; in secondary recorded as an open question, and other ready work goes on.
rule TriageForUser {
    when: Triage(y)
    requires: y.kind = user_decision or y.kind = invalidating_finding
    requires: not y.changes_goal and not y.changes_target
    ensures: y.triaged = true
    ensures:
        for sub_goal in y.bears_on:
            sub_goal.blocked_by_question = true
    ensures:
        if y.session.mode = primary:
            UserAsked(session: y.session, about: y)
        else:
            Recorded(session: y.session, entry: open_question)
}

rule UserSettles {
    when: UserAnswers(y)
    requires: y.kind = user_decision or y.kind = invalidating_finding
    ensures: Recorded(session: y.session, entry: decision)
    ensures:
        for sub_goal in y.bears_on:
            sub_goal.blocked_by_question = false
    ensures:
        if y.from_dart != null:
            OrchestratorResumes(dart: y.from_dart)

    @guidance
        -- An answer that changes the goal or the target is a new yield, triaged as one. The decision reaches the issue as Record and conduct says.
}

-- The orchestrator harvests the report: each finding in it is a Yield, triaged like any other,
-- and the dart's branch and head are recorded while its code is still evidence.
rule DartReports {
    when: DartReport(dart)
    requires: dart.status = running or dart.status = waiting
    ensures: dart.status = reported
    ensures: Recorded(session: dart.session, entry: harvest)

    @guidance
        -- Harvest into the plan and journal, adding a task for each sub-goal: the walking skeleton the dart stood up; the sub-goals, each with kind, dependencies and whether the dart exercised it, shaped as review units and together completing the card; the decisions you keep; the questions with what each blocks; the assumptions and risks, including each invariant the dart broke; the data structures that matched the problem; and the dart's branch and head.
        -- A sub-goal not exercised is darted before it is driven, unless you judge it straightforward (JudgeStraightforward).
}

rule DartHarvested {
    when: dart: c/Dart.status transitions_to reported
    ensures:
        if dart.still_needed:
            dart.status = evidence
        else:
            dart.status = cleared
}

-- A cleared dart's branch and worktree are always removed, whatever the project's branch-cleanup answer.
rule DartCleared {
    when: dart: c/Dart.still_needed becomes false
    requires: dart.status = evidence
    ensures: dart.status = cleared
    ensures: Recorded(session: dart.session, entry: what_it_taught)

    @guidance
        -- Keep a dart's branch only while its code is still evidence a drive needs; then record what it taught that the record doesn't yet hold.
}

-- A tidy needed first, main-bound or not, or an amend of a commit the frame in hand can't be committed on, interrupts the frame in hand:
-- the new frame goes on the stack, and the interrupted frame's task is blocked by it.
-- The pushed sub-goal was recorded, and its task added, when triage created it — a tidy needed first arrives as a sub_goal yield.
rule Interrupt {
    when: PushFrame(frame, sub_goal)
    requires: frame.status = writing
    requires: sub_goal.status = pending or sub_goal.status = ready
    ensures: frame.status = interrupted
    ensures: sub_goal.status = in_hand
    ensures: Recorded(session: frame.session, entry: wip_commit)
    ensures: c/Frame.created(
        session: frame.session, sub_goal: sub_goal, kind: sub_goal.kind,
        placement: sub_goal.placement, status: in_hand, interrupts: frame)
    ensures: TaskBlocked(task: frame.sub_goal, by: sub_goal)

    @guidance
        -- The new frame is committed by a drive agent of its own; the agent in hand WIP-commits before ending its turn; rebase that afterwards, or discard it to redo on the new structure, whichever is cheaper. A frame can itself be interrupted.
}

-- A better drive found mid-drive pre-empts the one in hand: the original is kept, not failed.
rule PreEmpt {
    when: BetterDrive(frame, sub_goal)
    requires: frame.status = writing
    requires: sub_goal.status = ready
    ensures: frame.status = pre_empted
    ensures: sub_goal.status = in_hand
    ensures: Recorded(session: frame.session, entry: wip_commit)
    ensures: TaskBlocked(task: frame.sub_goal, by: sub_goal)
    ensures: c/Frame.created(
        session: frame.session, sub_goal: sub_goal, kind: sub_goal.kind,
        placement: sub_goal.placement, status: in_hand, interrupts: frame)

    @guidance
        -- Set the original aside as a WIP commit, commit the pre-empting drive, then rebase the WIP onto it and resume the original with what is left.
}

-- A drive has failed once its change is no longer atomic — more than one change — or no longer comprehensible — more than a reviewer can hold: thrown away, re-split.
rule Fail {
    when: DriveFailed(frame)
    requires: frame.status = writing or frame.status = verifying
    ensures: frame.status = failed
    ensures: frame.sub_goal.status = pending
    ensures: TaskRemoved(sub_goal: frame.sub_goal)
    ensures:
        if frame.interrupts != null:
            frame.interrupts.status = writing

    @guidance
        -- Other signs of failure: the same failure twice, a fix reaching into code the sub-goal didn't name, a test you'd have to weaken, the code not being what the plan assumed.
        -- Step back: WIP-commit it or `git reset --hard` to the recorded head, mark the sub-goal not ready and remove its task; it is readied again once its re-split's tidies are committed, or superseded by the sub-goals that replace it.
        -- Break it down again per Tidy First?'s 'Getting Untangled' — by default discard the drive and redo it tidying first, with what it taught; dart, from where the work is and with what the drive taught, where the route is what's unknown; take it to the user where a decision is, recording it as an open question in secondary.
}

-- Drives run one at a time: one frame is being worked; the rest of the stack is interrupted or pre-empted under it.
invariant OneFrameInHand {
    for session in Sessions:
        session.in_hand.count <= 1
}

invariant DrivenOnlyWhenDrivable {
    for frame in Frames:
        frame.interrupts = null
            implies frame.sub_goal.exercised or frame.sub_goal.judged_straightforward
}
